title: What If You Swallow Gum?
voice: am_michael
speed: 1.0
lead: 0.4
outro: 0.9
gap: 0.28
music: bed
sources[2]: Mayo Clinic, 'Is it true that gum stays in your stomach for seven years if you swallow it?' (gum base is not digested; it passes through, usually within days), Milov et al., Pediatrics 1998, 'Chewing gum bezoars of the gastrointestinal tract' (large amounts swallowed often, mostly by children, can cause blockages)
lines[11]:
  - text, caps
  - What happens if you swallow your gum?, WHAT *HAPPENS | IF YOU *SWALLOW | YOUR *GUM?
  - "Well, it slides down your throat and lands right in your stomach,", "WELL, IT *SLIDES | DOWN YOUR *THROAT | RIGHT IN YOUR *STOMACH"
  - where strong acid gets to work on it., WHERE STRONG *ACID | GETS TO *WORK
  - "The sugar and flavor dissolve pretty quickly, but the gum base is a different story.", SUGAR *DISSOLVES | BUT THE *BASE | IS *DIFFERENT
  - "It's a rubbery polymer, and your body simply can't break it down.", RUBBERY *POLYMER | YOUR BODY *CAN'T | BREAK IT *DOWN
  - "So does it stay there for seven years? Nope, that's a total myth.", STAY 7 *YEARS? | *NOPE | TOTAL *MYTH
  - "Your gut just keeps pushing it along,", YOUR *GUT | KEEPS IT *MOVING
  - "and within a few days, it's out of your system.", A FEW *DAYS | IT'S *OUT
  - "But if you swallow a lot of gum all at once,", BUT *IF... | SWALLOW A *LOT | ALL AT *ONCE
  - it can clump together and actually block your gut., IT CAN *CLUMP | AND *BLOCK | YOUR *GUT
  - "So maybe, just spit it out.", SO *MAYBE... | SPIT IT *OUT
